{
  "updated": "2026-11-09",
  "slug": "what-is-a-fiduciary",
  "title": "What Is a Fiduciary and Which Advisors Must Act for You",
  "metaDescription": "A fiduciary must act in your best interest. See which standard binds advisers, brokers and Certified Financial Planner professionals, and how to check.",
  "h1": "What Is a Fiduciary?",
  "cardBlurb": "Which standard applies to an investment adviser, a broker-dealer or a Certified Financial Planner professional, and how to check before you hire.",
  "introText": "A fiduciary is a professional who must act in your best interest, and some financial advisors are required to be one. At ModernWallet, we answer a question like what is a fiduciary with the rule itself and the name of the body that wrote it.\n\nThree rules decide who owes you what. The [Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)](https://www.sec.gov/newsroom/speeches-statements/clayton-regulation-best-interest-investment-adviser-fiduciary-duty) holds registered investment advisers to a fiduciary duty and holds broker-dealers to a separate standard called Regulation Best Interest. A Certified Financial Planner (CFP) professional must act as a fiduciary whenever giving financial advice to a client.\n\nMany firms are registered as both a broker and an adviser, so the person you hire may offer accounts under either rule. The firm's customer or client relationship summary, called [Form CRS](https://www.investor.gov/CRS), states the standard of conduct tied to its services.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "What a Fiduciary Is and Who Has to Be One",
      "body": "A fiduciary is a professional who must act in your best interest and may not put their own interest ahead of yours. [Investor.gov](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/working-investment-professional/investment-advisers) states the rule for investment advisers this way: advisers \"are required to act in your best interest and not put their interest ahead of yours.\" The rule reads differently for investment advisers, broker-dealers and CFP professionals.\n\nRegistered investment advisers owe a fiduciary duty with two parts. In a July 8, 2019 statement, SEC Chairman Jay Clayton said the duty is \"comprised of both a duty of care and a duty of loyalty.\" It requires an adviser to act in the client's best interest and not subordinate that interest to the adviser's own.\n\nInvestor.gov says advisers typically give ongoing advice about buying, selling or holding investments. They also monitor how those investments perform and whether they still fit your overall objectives.\n\nBroker-dealers follow Regulation Best Interest, which the same SEC statement says \"imposes a new standard of conduct specifically for broker-dealers.\" The rule requires a broker to act in the \"best interest of their retail customers\" and to meet four component obligations:\n\n- Disclosure\n- Care\n- Conflict of Interest\n- Compliance\n\nThe SEC statement presents Regulation Best Interest as its own standard for brokers, apart from the adviser fiduciary duty.\n\nCFP professionals take on a fiduciary duty through certification. The [Certified Financial Planner Board of Standards (CFP Board)](https://www.cfp.net/ethics/code-of-ethics-and-standards-of-conduct) Code of Ethics and Standards of Conduct says: \"At all times when providing Financial Advice to a Client, a CFP professional must act as a fiduciary, and therefore, act in the best interests of the Client.\"\n\nThe CFP Board describes the ethics requirement as the final step on the path to certification, when the candidate signs the Ethics Declaration. Our [guide to the CFP credential](/guides/what-is-a-cfp/) explains what the certification involves."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Which Standard Applies to an Adviser, Broker or Dual Registrant",
      "body": "The standard that applies depends on how the professional and the firm are registered, and the firm's Form CRS states it in writing. Investor.gov says both brokers and advisers can give advice about buying investments like stock in a company or an index fund. Both can also advise on whether to sell or hold afterward, so the kind of advice you receive does not tell you which rule covers it.\n\n| Professional | Standard named by the source | Where to see it |\n|---|---|---|\n| SEC-registered investment adviser | Fiduciary duty of care and loyalty (SEC statement, Investor.gov) | The firm's Form CRS, which states the required standard of conduct |\n| Broker-dealer | Regulation Best Interest, with Disclosure, Care, Conflict of Interest and Compliance obligations (SEC statement) | The firm's Form CRS |\n| Dual registrant (registered as both broker and adviser) | The standard tied to each service the firm offers | The firm's Form CRS, which lists services and the standard for each |\n| CFP professional | Must act as a fiduciary when providing financial advice (CFP Board Code) | The [CFP Board verification page](https://www.cfp.net/verify-a-cfp-professional) |\n\nInvestor.gov says many firms are registered as both a broker and an adviser. These dual registrants offer both advisory and brokerage services and accounts.\n\nInvestor.gov adds that a combination may be a good choice in some cases, depending on your goals, preferences and needs. If you open more than one account at a dual registrant, ask which standard covers each account."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Conflicts and Titles That a Fiduciary Duty Leaves in Place",
      "body": "A fiduciary duty does not remove conflicts of interest, and Investor.gov says so on its investment adviser page. Right after stating the best-interest rule, the page adds: \"At the same time, the way advisers make money creates some conflicts with your interests.\" Investor.gov says you should understand these conflicts and ask an adviser about them, because they can affect the advice you get.\n\nPay is the first place to look for a conflict. Investor.gov says an adviser's fee is typically based on the value of all the assets in your account with that adviser. You may also pay other fees and costs for servicing the account and for the investments you buy, sell or hold.\n\nThe firm's Form CRS lists its fees and costs and its conflicts of interest. It also says whether the firm and its financial professionals have reportable legal or disciplinary history.\n\nTitles do not prove fiduciary status. Investor.gov's \"Tricky Titles\" guidance says not to assume that initials after a broker's or adviser's name make that person more qualified than another. The titles are not all the same, and some initials mean the person can only sell certain products.\n\nInvestor.gov also points to a list showing that some credentials are easy to obtain while others are hard-earned. The Certified Trust and Fiduciary Advisor (CTFA) is a separate credential from the American Bankers Association (ABA), covered in our [CTFA guide](/guides/what-is-a-ctfa/). Whatever the title, look up the person's registration at [adviserinfo.sec.gov](https://adviserinfo.sec.gov) before relying on the letters."
    },
    {
      "heading": "How to Check a Financial Professional Before You Hire",
      "body": "Registration is the first thing to check, because Investor.gov says the most important question before hiring an investment professional is whether the person is registered with the SEC or a state securities regulator. Investor.gov also says it is \"really risky to invest with someone who isn't licensed.\" Four checks cover the standards described here:\n\n1. Search an adviser's name at [adviserinfo.sec.gov](https://adviserinfo.sec.gov) and a broker's name at [BrokerCheck](https://brokercheck.finra.org), run by the Financial Industry Regulatory Authority (FINRA).\n2. Confirm any CFP claim on the [CFP Board's verification page](https://www.cfp.net/verify-a-cfp-professional).\n3. Ask for the firm's [Form CRS](https://www.investor.gov/CRS) and read its standard of conduct, fees and costs, conflicts of interest and disciplinary history. Investor.gov says these summaries use similar headings in the same order, so you can lay two firms' summaries side by side.\n4. Ask in writing which standard applies to your account: the adviser fiduciary duty, Regulation Best Interest, or the CFP Board fiduciary requirement.\n\nInvestor.gov's guidance on selecting an adviser lists what to weigh before you choose:\n\n- What services and products you need\n- What services and products the adviser can provide, and any limits on them\n- How much you will pay for services and transactions\n- How the adviser gets paid\n- What conflicts of interest the adviser may have\n- Whether the adviser has legal or disciplinary history, and for what type of conduct\n\nForm CRS also includes conversation starters, which give you a ready list of questions for a first meeting. Our [guide to choosing a financial advisor](/guides/how-to-choose-a-financial-advisor/) walks through the rest of the decision."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Who a Fiduciary Is Not For and What Would Change the Answer",
      "body": "Ongoing fiduciary advice may be more than you need if you want a one-time answer or manage a simple portfolio on your own. Investor.gov describes the typical adviser arrangement as ongoing advice with monitoring, paid by a fee typically based on the value of the assets in your account. That arrangement fits someone who wants continuing oversight of their investments.\n\nIf you want help with a single decision, read a firm's Form CRS first to see which services it offers. Investor.gov notes that advisers may also give advice about market trends or asset allocation, or offer financial planning services.\n\nThis answer rests on published rules, so any of these changes would change it:\n\n- The SEC amends Regulation Best Interest or its interpretation of the adviser fiduciary duty.\n- The CFP Board changes its Code of Ethics and Standards of Conduct.\n- The person you are considering is registered only as a broker, so Regulation Best Interest applies instead of the adviser duty.\n- The person holds no registration, which Investor.gov calls \"really risky\" to invest with.\n\nIf your question is about job titles rather than legal standards, our [financial advisor vs. financial planner comparison](/compare/financial-advisor-vs-financial-planner/) covers the two titles."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Our Takeaway",
      "body": "A fiduciary must act in your best interest. An SEC-registered investment adviser owes that as a duty of care and loyalty, a broker-dealer owes the separate Regulation Best Interest standard, and a CFP professional commits to fiduciary duty in the CFP Board Code. This applies to anyone paying for investment advice.\n\nConflicts of interest remain under every one of these standards, so read the firm's Form CRS and ask about them. The answer changes if the person is registered only as a broker or holds neither registration.\n\nBefore you hire anyone, search the person on [adviserinfo.sec.gov](https://adviserinfo.sec.gov) or [BrokerCheck](https://brokercheck.finra.org), then ask in writing which standard applies to your account. Our [guide to choosing a financial advisor](/guides/how-to-choose-a-financial-advisor/) lists the next questions to ask."
    }
  ],
