  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "Is a financial advisor a fiduciary?",
      "answer": "A financial advisor is a fiduciary only if registered as an investment adviser or if the advisor is a CFP professional giving financial advice. A professional registered as a broker-dealer works under Regulation Best Interest, a separate SEC standard for brokers. Many firms are registered as both, so ask which standard applies to your account and read the firm's Form CRS."
    },
    {
      "question": "Is a fiduciary the same as a financial planner?",
      "answer": "Not necessarily. The SEC and Investor.gov sources cited here do not define \"financial planner\" as a title, and Investor.gov warns that titles and initials are not all the same. A CFP professional is different, because the CFP Board Code says a CFP professional must act as a fiduciary when providing financial advice to a client."
    },
    {
      "question": "Why is a fiduciary important?",
      "answer": "A fiduciary must act in your best interest and not put their own interest ahead of yours, according to Investor.gov. That duty does not remove conflicts of interest, since Investor.gov says the way advisers make money creates some conflicts with your interests. The firm's Form CRS lists those conflicts so you can ask about them before you hire."
    },
    {
      "question": "Who needs a fiduciary?",
      "answer": "Anyone paying for investment advice should know whether the person giving it owes a fiduciary duty or another standard. Investor.gov says advisers typically give ongoing advice and monitor your investments, which fits someone who wants continuing oversight. If you need a single answer or manage a simple portfolio yourself, ongoing advice may be more than you need."
    }
  ],
